One common vulnerability in Java web applications is **Insecure Deserialization**, often caused by using the `ObjectInputStream` class to deserialize untrusted data. This can allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code on the server via gadget chains.

```java
import java.io.*;

public class DeserializationVulnerability {
    // This method is vulnerable to Remote Code Execution (RCE) 
    // if an attacker provides a specially crafted serialized object.
    public void processData(byte[] inputData) throws IOException, ClassNotFoundException {
        ByteArrayInputStream bis = new ByteArrayInputStream(inputData);
        ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(bis);

        // Reading data from an untrusted source before validating the class type
        Object obj = ois.readObject(); 
        System.out.println("Processed: " + obj.toString());
    }
}
***
import java.io.*;
import org.apache.commons.io.SerializationUtils;

public class SecureDeserialization {
    // To fix this, we should avoid native Java serialization 
    // or use a look-ahead ObjectInputStream to whitelist allowed classes.
    // A better approach is using JSON/XML with a strict schema.
    public void processData(String jsonData) {
        // Example using a library like Jackson to deserialize JSON 
        // instead of using ObjectInputStream on raw binary data.
        // This prevents attackers from instulating arbitrary classes.
        MyDataPojo data = JSONUtils.fromJson(jsonData, MyDataPojo.class);
        System.out.println("Processed: " + data.toString());
    }
}

class MyDataPojo {
    private String name;
    public String getName() { return name; }
    public void setName(String name) { this.name = name; }
}
